---
import Page from "../components/Page.astro";

const spans = [
	["Day", "The last price against the close of the session before. While the market is open it is the day so far."],
	["Week", "The last close against the close five sessions before."],
	["Month", "Against the close 21 sessions before."],
	["YTD", "Against the last close of the year before."],
	["1Y", "Against the close 252 sessions before."],
];
---

<Page title="Method" description="Where the map comes from: the text it reads, how similarity is measured and how the price moves are counted.">
	<div class="max-w-3xl">
		<h1 class="text-[2rem] font-medium leading-tight tracking-tight text-ink-strong">Sources and method</h1>
		<p class="mt-4 text-ink">
			The map is computed by code, the same way for every company. No one places a company by hand, and no AI writes anything
			on these pages.
		</p>

		<h2 class="sec sec-title mt-12">Sources</h2>
		<ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-ink">
			<li><strong class="font-medium text-ink-strong">SEC EDGAR</strong>: the list of US-listed companies, each one's industry, and the text of its latest annual report (10-K, or 20-F for a foreign company).</li>
			<li><strong class="font-medium text-ink-strong">Yahoo Finance</strong>: daily closing prices, for the colour of the dots.</li>
			<li><strong class="font-medium text-ink-strong">BAAI bge-small-en-v1.5</strong>: an open text-embedding model, run on our own machine when the map is built.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2 class="sec sec-title mt-12">Which companies</h2>
		<p class="mt-3 text-ink">
			The first 1,725 companies of the SEC's list, which is ordered roughly by market value, that have a Business section to
			read: about seven in eight. The others are not on the map. Some file no annual report of these kinds (Canadian
			companies, and foreign ones traded over the counter); some lay their annual report out their own way, without a
			separate Business section.
		</p>

		<h2 class="sec sec-title mt-12">Similarity</h2>
		<p class="mt-3 text-ink">
			The text read is the Business section of the annual report (Item 1 of a 10-K, Item 4 of a 20-F): what the company itself
			says it does. The model turns it into a vector: the average over its passages, minus the average of all companies, so
			that what every annual report says does not count. The similarity of two companies is the cosine between their vectors:
			1 would be the same text, 0 nothing in common beyond what all reports share. Each company keeps its ten nearest.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-3 text-ink">
			Similar means the texts are alike. Two companies can describe the same business and differ in size, results and
			price; a bank's nearest are often banks that write about the same regulation. It says nothing about which company is
			better, and it is not a list of alternatives to buy.
		</p>

		<h2 class="sec sec-title mt-12">The map</h2>
		<p class="mt-3 text-ink">
			A projection of those vectors onto a plane (t-SNE). Dots that are close are similar; the axes, and distances between
			far-apart regions, mean nothing. The names on the map are the SEC industry that most companies of an area are filed
			under. The map is rebuilt by hand, so a new annual report counts from the next rebuild.
		</p>

		<h2 class="sec sec-title mt-12">Price moves</h2>
		<p class="mt-3 text-ink">
			The colour of a dot is the change in the company's share price over the span chosen, without dividends: green up, red
			down, grey around zero, stronger the bigger the move. The full colour is reached at 3% for a day, 6% for a week, 12% for
			a month, 40% for the year to date and 60% for a year; beyond that the colour does not change, the figure does.
		</p>
		<dl class="mt-2 divide-y divide-line">
			{spans.map(([term, text]) => (
				<div class="grid gap-1 py-3 sm:grid-cols-[8rem_1fr] sm:gap-6">
					<dt class="text-ink-strong">{term}</dt>
					<dd class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{text}</dd>
				</div>
			))}
		</dl>
		<p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted">
			Prices are read again when someone opens the map and finds them more than fifteen minutes old with the market open, and
			once after the close. They can be delayed. A foreign company's price is that of its shares traded in the US.
		</p>

		<h2 class="sec sec-title mt-12">What this is not</h2>
		<p class="mt-3 text-ink">
			This site describes texts and past prices. It does not recommend buying, selling or holding any security, does not set
			price targets and does not forecast returns. That a company's neighbours rose, or that it fell while they did not, is
			what happened, not a sign of what comes next. Data from third parties may be delayed or contain errors.
		</p>
	</div>
</Page>
